chunks.x:
Generic lambdas: auto in a lambda's parameter list, letting one lambda work across multiple types
Relaxed constexpr rules: constexpr functions could now contain loops and multiple statements, not just a single return expression
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    auto add = [](auto a, auto b) { return a + b; }; // generic lambda, C++14
    std::cout << add(2, 3) << "\n";       // works with ints
    std::cout << add(2.5, 1.5) << "\n";   // and doubles, same lambda
}
```

Structured bindings: unpack a pair/tuple/struct into named variables in one line
if with an initializer: if (auto it = m.find(key); it != m.end())
std::optional, std::variant, std::any: safer alternatives to nullable pointers and unsafe unions
Fold expressions for variadic templates (covered in the templates chapter)
```
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <optional>

std::optional<int> find(const std::map<std::string, int>& m, const std::string& key) {
    if (auto it = m.find(key); it != m.end()) { // if-with-initializer
        return it->second;
    }
    return std::nullopt;
}

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> ages{{"Ada", 36}, {"Grace", 85}};
    if (auto age = find(ages, "Ada")) { // std::optional is truthy when it holds a value
        std::cout << "found: " << *age << "\n";
    }

    auto [key, value] = *ages.begin(); // structured binding
    std::cout << key << " -> " << value << "\n";
}
```

Concepts (covered in the templates chapter): named, readable template constraints
Ranges: composable operations over sequences, e.g. std::views::filter and std::views::transform, without hand-writing loops or intermediate containers
The spaceship operator <=>: write one comparison and get all six relational operators generated for you
Coroutines: language support for suspendable functions (co_await, co_yield). Powerful, but the library-level ergonomics (writing your own coroutine types) are still fiddly; most people use coroutines through a library that wraps the hard parts rather than building the machinery by hand
Modules: an alternative to the #include/header-file model, meant to speed up compilation and avoid macro leakage. Compiler and build-system support is still catching up as of this writing, so don't be surprised if module support varies noticeably between toolchains
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <ranges>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8};
    auto evenSquares = nums
        | std::views::filter([](int n) { return n % 2 == 0; })
        | std::views::transform([](int n) { return n * n; });

    for (int n : evenSquares) std::cout << n << " ";
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <ranges>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> words{"a", "banana", "cat", "dinosaur", "ox"};

    auto longWords = words
        | std::views::filter([](const std::string& w) { return w.size() > 2; });

    for (const auto& w : longWords) std::cout << w << "\n";
}
```

The function templates chapter earlier in this course showed this already: a single generic function or class definition gets a distinct, fully concrete version generated for each type it's used with, decided entirely at compile time. There's no runtime cost, no virtual dispatch, no indirection, the compiler picks the right code to run before the program ever executes.
```
template<typename T>
T double_it(T x) { return x + x; }

int main() {
    double_it(5);     // generates an int version at compile time
    double_it(2.5);   // generates a separate double version at compile time
    return 0;
}
```

Inheritance and virtual functions (both covered in depth later in this course) solve a different problem: calling the *right* version of a function through a common base-class pointer or reference, when the actual concrete type isn't known until the program is running, maybe it depends on user input, a config file, or which subclass got constructed somewhere else entirely.
```
#include <iostream>

struct Shape {
    virtual double area() const = 0;
    virtual ~Shape() = default;
};

struct Circle : Shape {
    double radius;
    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }
};

struct Square : Shape {
    double side;
    double area() const override { return side * side; }
};

int main() {
    Circle circle{2.0};
    Square square{3.0};
    Shape* shapes[] = {&circle, &square};
    for (Shape* s : shapes) {
        std::cout << s->area() << "\n"; // the RIGHT area() runs for each, decided at runtime
    }
}
```

C++, End to End
const and constexpr both promise a value won't change after initialization, but they promise it at different times, and mixing them up leads to real confusion about what the compiler can and can't do with a value.
c++
```
const int a = 5;        // cannot be reassigned; a's value might still only be known at runtime
constexpr int b = 5;    // cannot be reassigned, AND must be computable at compile time
```
```
#include <iostream>

int getUserAge();  // imagine this reads from a file or asks the user

int main() {
    const int age = getUserAge();      // fine: const just means "won't change again"
    // constexpr int age2 = getUserAge();  // error: not knowable at compile time
    std::cout << "const compiles fine here\n";
}

int getUserAge() { return 30; }
```
Reach for const by default on any variable you don't intend to modify after initialization, which in well-written C++ is most of them: it documents intent, and it lets the compiler catch an accidental reassignment as an error instead of a silent bug. Reach for constexpr specifically when the value is known at compile time and you want the compiler to be able to use it in contexts that require that (array sizes, template arguments, other constexpr computations).

A C-style string is just a pointer to the first char of a sequence terminated by a null byte ('\0'), and it inherits every sharp edge that comes with manual memory and manual bounds (a full treatment of those specific footguns, buffer overflows and off-by-one termination bugs, already lives in this site's C course, since C strings are a C concept C++ inherited). C++ gives you a real alternative: std::string, from <string>, which owns its own memory, tracks its own length, resizes itself automatically, and is the type you should reach for by default.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name = "Ada";
    name += " Lovelace";              // grows automatically, no manual buffer management
    std::cout << name << "\n";
    std::cout << "length: " << name.length() << "\n";
    std::cout << "first char: " << name[0] << "\n";
}
```
A few operations worth knowing immediately because they come up constantly: comparing two strings with ==, concatenating with + or +=, checking emptiness with.empty() (faster and clearer than checking.length() == 0), and.substr(start, count) for extracting a piece.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string a = "hello";
    std::string b = "hello";
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << (a == b) << "\n";   // true, compares content

    std::string full = "hello world";
    std::cout << full.substr(6, 5) << "\n";              // "world"
}
```
std::string can still hand you a raw C-style string when an older API demands one, via.c_str(), which returns a pointer valid only as long as the std::string itself isn't modified or destroyed afterward. That's a real lifetime hazard worth remembering, but it's an edge case you reach for, not the default way of working with text.

A constexpr variable's value must be computable at compile time, and the compiler enforces that: if you try to initialize one with something that isn't knowable until runtime, it's a compile error, not a runtime one.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    constexpr int max_players = 4;
    int scores[max_players]; // fine: max_players is a compile-time constant

    int runtime_value = 4;
    // int bad[runtime_value]; // not standard C++, this needs a compile-time constant

    std::cout << max_players << "\n";
}
```
This is stricter than plain const. A const int can be initialized from a runtime value (const int x = some_function(); compiles fine, x just can't be reassigned afterward). constexpr additionally demands the value be known at compile time.

A function marked constexpr *can* run at compile time, if all of its arguments are compile-time constants and its body is simple enough (no undefined behavior, since the compiler has to evaluate it). Called with runtime values instead, the exact same function just runs normally at runtime. It's one function definition serving both cases.
```
#include <iostream>

constexpr int factorial(int n) {
    return (n <= 1) ? 1 : n * factorial(n - 1);
}

int main() {
    constexpr int compile_time_result = factorial(5); // computed during compilation
    std::cout << compile_time_result << "\n";

    int n;
    std::cin >> n; // hypothetically: a value only known at runtime
    // factorial(n) here would run as a normal recursive function call instead
    std::cout << factorial(10) << "\n";
}
```
"Can run at compile time" is not the same as "always runs at compile time". Whether a particular call gets evaluated at compile time depends on context (is the result being used somewhere that demands a compile-time constant, like an array bound or another constexpr variable's initializer) and, in some cases, is left up to the compiler to decide as an optimization.

C++20 added consteval, which is stricter still: a consteval function (an "immediate function") must always be evaluated at compile time. Calling it with anything that isn't a compile-time constant is a compile error, not a fallback to runtime execution.
```
// godbolt-run -std=c++20
consteval int square(int x) {
    return x * x;
}

int main() {
    constexpr int result = square(5); // fine, compile-time
    // int n;
    // std::cin >> n;
    // int bad = square(n); // compile error: n isn't known at compile time
    return result;
}
```
Use consteval when a function only makes sense at compile time (generating a lookup table baked into the binary, validating a format string) and you want the compiler to guarantee that rather than silently falling back to a runtime call.

Ordinary if picks a branch at runtime, but both branches still have to compile even if one is never taken for a given type. if constexpr (C++17) discards the untaken branch entirely at compile time, which matters most inside templates, where the discarded branch might not even be valid code for the type being used.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>

template<typename T>
void describe(T value) {
    if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
        std::cout << value << " is an integer\n";
    } else if constexpr (std::is_floating_point_v<T>) {
        std::cout << value << " is a floating-point number\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "some other type\n";
    }
}

int main() {
    describe(42);
    describe(3.14);
}
```

Covered in more depth in the error-handling chapter, but worth repeating here since it's the natural companion to everything above: static_assert(condition, "message") fails the *build* if condition isn't true, checked entirely at compile time. It's the standard way to validate assumptions about constexpr computations, type sizes, or template parameters before the program ever runs.
Try it yourself: make a runtime function compile-time capable›
This function works fine, but it can only ever run at runtime. Add constexpr to its signature, then declare a constexpr variable that calls it, forcing compile-time evaluation, and confirm it still works correctly when called with a runtime value too.
```
#include <iostream>

int sum_up_to(int n) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) total += i;
    return total;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << sum_up_to(10) << "\n";
}
```

Standard stuff: if (cond) { } optionally followed by else if chains and a final else. The condition just needs to be contextually convertible to bool, which is looser than it sounds: pointers, integers, and anything with an explicit operator bool() all qualify. That looseness is also a classic source of bugs, if (x = 5) compiles fine and assigns 5 to x, then branches on whether 5 is truthy (it is), when you almost certainly meant ==.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x = 0;
    if (x = 5) { // assignment, not comparison
        std::cout << "this always runs, x is now " << x << "\n";
    }
}
```
Most compilers warn on this with -Wall (-Wparentheses). Treat that warning as an error in your own projects; there is essentially never a legitimate reason to write if (x = y) on purpose without wrapping it in extra parens to signal intent.

switch compares one value against a set of constant case labels. The part that trips people up coming from other languages: cases fall through by default. Execution continues into the next case unless you break.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int day = 3;
    switch (day) {
        case 1:
        case 2:
        case 3:
        case 4:
        case 5:
            std::cout << "weekday\n";
            break;
        case 6:
        case 7:
            std::cout << "weekend\n";
            break;
        default:
            std::cout << "not a valid day\n";
    }
}
```
Stacking case labels with no code between them (like 1: through 5: above) is the one place fallthrough is useful: it lets several labels share one block. Falling through *into* a block that has its own statements is the dangerous kind, and it usually means a missing break. If you ever want intentional fallthrough into a non-empty case, mark it with the [[fallthrough]]; attribute (C++17) so the compiler and the next reader both know it was deliberate rather than a bug.

while (cond) { } checks the condition before each iteration, so the body might run zero times. do { } while (cond); checks after, so the body always runs at least once, useful for things like "read input, then keep reading while it's valid". The classic for (init; cond; step) { } is just a while loop with the bookkeeping baked into the header.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        std::cout << i << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

C++11 added a for that iterates a container or array directly, without manual indexing:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for (int n : nums) {
        std::cout << n << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << "\n";

    // use a reference to modify in place, and const auto& to avoid copies
    for (auto& n : nums) n *= 2;
    for (const auto& n : nums) std::cout << n << ' ';
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```
Default to const auto& when you're only reading, auto& when you need to mutate, and plain auto (by value) only when the elements are cheap to copy or you want a copy. Copying every element of a large container just to read it is a common, easy-to-miss performance mistake.

Sometimes the natural loop condition needs to check something that's only known partway through the loop body, classically: read a line, then loop while the read succeeded. A while loop can't express "check after reading, but before doing anything else with what was read" cleanly. Two common patterns:
An infinite loop (while (true)) with an explicit if (...) break; where the natural exit condition becomes known.
Restructuring so the check-then-use both happen inside the loop condition itself, e.g. while (std::getline(input, line)) { ... }, which works because std::getline returns a stream convertible to bool.
Try it yourself: fix the fallthrough bug›
This switch statement is supposed to print a grade letter based on a score, but it prints multiple lines for some inputs. Find the missing break and fix it, then verify with a few different score values.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int score = 85;
    switch (score / 10) {
        case 10:
        case 9:
            std::cout << "A\n";
        case 8:
            std::cout << "B\n";
        case 7:
            std::cout << "C\n";
        default:
            std::cout << "F\n";
    }
}
```

Compiler output reads bottom-to-top less often than you'd think for C++; usually the first error listed is the real one, and everything after it is the compiler getting confused by the fallout from that first error. Fix the first error, recompile, and see how much of the rest disappears before chasing the second one.
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x = 5
    std::cout << x << "\n";
}
```
Run that: a missing semicolon after `int x = 5` produces a real error, but notice where the compiler reports it. It often points at the following line, since that's where it first found something that didn't fit its expectations.
Warnings deserve equal attention. -Wall -Wextra (on GCC and Clang) turn on the checks that catch the most common real bugs: comparing signed and unsigned integers, an unused variable, a switch statement missing a case, a function that might not return on every path. A codebase that compiles clean with -Wall -Wextra -Werror (the last flag turns warnings into hard errors) catches an enormous number of bugs before they ever run.

An assertion states something you believe must always be true at that point in the program, and crashes immediately (with a message naming the file and line) if it isn't. assert lives in <cassert>.
```
#include <cassert>
#include <iostream>

double safeDivide(double numerator, double denominator) {
    assert(denominator != 0.0 && "safeDivide: denominator must not be zero");
    return numerator / denominator;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << safeDivide(10, 2) << "\n";
    std::cout << safeDivide(10, 0) << "\n";  // assertion fails here
}
```
An assertion is not error handling. It doesn't recover, it doesn't let the caller do anything, it just stops the program with a clear message pointing at the exact broken assumption. That's precisely the point: it's a tool for catching your own bugs during development, not for handling bad input a real user might legitimately provide (that's what actual error handling, covered in a later chapter, is for). In release builds, NDEBUG typically strips asserts out entirely, so never put code with side effects inside one; if the assert vanishes, that code vanishes with it.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> a;              // empty
    std::vector<int> b(5);           // 5 elements, each value-initialized to 0
    std::vector<int> c(5, 42);       // 5 elements, each set to 42
    std::vector<int> d{1, 2, 3, 4};  // list-initialized with these exact values

    std::cout << a.size() << ", " << b.size() << ", " << c.size() << ", " << d.size() << "\n";
}
```
std::vector<int> v(5, 42); and std::vector<int> v{5, 42}; do different things: the first constructs 5 elements each equal to 42, the second constructs a 2-element vector containing exactly 5 and 42. Braces prefer the initializer-list constructor when one exists and the types fit. This is a common source of confusion, worth checking any time you write vector construction with two numeric arguments.

size() is how many elements the vector holds. capacity() is how much memory it's currently reserved, which is often larger than size() to avoid reallocating on every single push. When capacity is exhausted, the vector allocates a new, larger block (usually growing geometrically, like doubling), copies or moves every existing element over, and frees the old block. That's an O(n) operation, but because it happens rarely as size grows, the amortized cost of a single push_back is still O(1).
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v;
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        v.push_back(i);
        std::cout << "size=" << v.size() << " capacity=" << v.capacity() << "\n";
    }
}
```
Run that and watch capacity jump in steps, not one at a time. The exact growth factor isn't specified by the standard (implementations commonly use 1.5x or 2x), so don't write code that depends on the specific numbers you see, just on the general shape.

push_back takes an already-constructed object (or something convertible to one) and copies or moves it into the vector. emplace_back takes the constructor arguments directly and builds the object in place, inside the vector's storage, with no separate temporary object and no copy or move of it.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Point {
    int x, y;
    Point(int x_, int y_) : x(x_), y(y_) {
        std::cout << "constructed (" << x << "," << y << ")\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    std::vector<Point> v;
    v.reserve(2); // avoid growth-related extra moves complicating this example

    v.push_back(Point(1, 2));  // constructs a temporary Point, then moves it in
    v.emplace_back(3, 4);      // constructs the Point directly inside the vector
}
```
For a type this small the difference is academic. For expensive-to-move types, or types with no move constructor at all, emplace_back avoiding the extra construction can matter. It's a reasonable default habit, but not something to obsess over: correctness first, this optimization second.

Any operation that might reallocate (push_back, insert, reserve past current capacity) can invalidate every iterator, pointer, and reference into the vector, because the whole buffer may have moved. Even operations that don't reallocate, like erase, invalidate iterators from the erased point onward, because everything after shifts down to fill the gap.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    // WRONG: modifying the vector you're iterating over
    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
        if (*it == 3) {
            v.push_back(99); // may reallocate, invalidating "it" mid-loop
        }
    }
    // The above has undefined behavior if a reallocation happens during the loop.
    // Don't run this expecting a predictable result.
}
```
The safe pattern for "remove things matching a condition while iterating" is to let erase tell you where the next valid iterator is, since it returns one:

```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};

    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) {
        if (*it % 2 == 0) {
            it = v.erase(it); // erase returns an iterator to the element after the one removed
        } else {
            ++it;
        }
    }

    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << "\n"; // 1 3 5
}
```
For this specific pattern, the standard library's erase-remove idiom (or std::erase/std::erase_if, added in C++20 as free functions) is usually cleaner still, but understanding the manual version above is what makes the shortcut make sense rather than feel like a magic incantation.
Try it yourself: fix the reallocation bug›
This function is supposed to double every element that's still below a threshold, appending the doubled value, but it has undefined behavior for the same reason as the broken loop above. Rewrite it so it's well-defined, either by not growing the vector while iterating it, or by using indices instead of iterators in a way that's still correct once growth happens.

c++
```
std::vector<int> growSmallValues(std::vector<int> v, int threshold) {
    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
        if (*it < threshold) {
            v.push_back(*it * 2); // may reallocate and invalidate "it"
        }
    }
    return v;
}
```
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> growSmallValues(std::vector<int> v, int threshold) {
    // Fix: capture the original size first, then loop by index only up to that
    // original size. New elements pushed on the end are never re-visited, and
    // indices stay valid across reallocation (unlike iterators).
    std::size_t originalSize = v.size();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < originalSize; ++i) {
        if (v[i] < threshold) {
            v.push_back(v[i] * 2);
        }
    }
    return v;
}

int main() {
    auto result = growSmallValues({1, 5, 2, 8, 3}, 4);
    for (int x : result) std::cout << x << " ";
…
```

```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int* p = new int(42);   // allocates one int on the heap, initialized to 42
    std::cout << *p << "\n";
    delete p;                // frees it; "p" is now a dangling pointer, don't use it again

    int* arr = new int[5]{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; // allocates an array of 5 ints
    std::cout << arr[2] << "\n";           // 3
    delete[] arr;                            // note: delete[], not delete, for array allocations
}
```
Using plain delete on something allocated with new[], or delete[] on something allocated with plain new, is undefined behavior. The two must always be paired correctly. This mismatch is exactly the kind of easy-to-miss bug that's a strong argument for not writing manual new/delete in the first place.

Every object you allocate with new has to be freed with exactly one matching delete, on every code path, including ones that exit early, throw an exception, or return in the middle of a function. Miss one, and you leak memory. Free the same pointer twice, or use it after freeing it, and you get undefined behavior that might not show up until much later, in a completely unrelated part of the program.
```
#include <iostream>

void processMightThrow(bool shouldThrow) {
    int* buffer = new int[1000];

    if (shouldThrow) {
        throw std::runtime_error("something went wrong");
        // "buffer" is never freed on this path: it leaks, because the throw
        // skips straight past the delete[] below.
    }

    delete[] buffer;
}
```
std::vector, std::string, and smart pointers (covered in this course's dedicated move-semantics chapter) all solve exactly this problem using RAII: the object's constructor acquires the resource, and its destructor releases it, and destructors run automatically during stack unwinding, including when an exception is thrown. Wrap the raw resource in one of these, and the leak above becomes structurally impossible instead of something you have to remember to avoid.

```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>

void processMightThrow(bool shouldThrow) {
    std::vector<int> buffer(1000); // owns its memory; frees it automatically, always

    if (shouldThrow) {
        throw std::runtime_error("something went wrong");
        // buffer's destructor still runs during stack unwinding here. No leak.
    }
}

int main() {
    try {
        processMightThrow(true);
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "caught: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }
}
```

```
#include <iostream>

void leaky() {
    int* data = new int[100];
    // ... work with data ...
    // forgot delete[] data; -- this block of memory is now unreachable and unrecoverable
    // until the program exits. Do this in a loop and memory usage climbs unbounded.
}
```
```
#include <iostream>

void doubleFree() {
    int* p = new int(5);
    delete p;
    // ... much later, in code that doesn't realize p was already freed ...
    delete p; // undefined behavior: freeing already-freed memory
}
```
Both of these compile without warning or error in general, and neither necessarily crashes immediately. That delay between the mistake and its visible effect is exactly why these bugs are so much harder to track down than a syntax error, and exactly why the RAII-based alternative isn't just "nicer", it removes the entire bug category.

Placement new constructs an object at a specific, already-allocated memory address, instead of allocating new memory itself: new (address) Type(args). It's a advanced, rare tool, used mostly by people implementing their own memory allocators, object pools, or low-level containers (in fact, this is exactly how std::vector itself constructs elements into its pre-allocated storage internally). You're very unlikely to need it in application code; it's mentioned here so the name doesn't come as a surprise if you encounter it, not as something to reach for.
Try it yourself: find and fix the leak›
This function leaks memory on one specific path. Find it, then fix it, first with a manual delete, and then again the better way (removing manual memory management from this function entirely).
c++
```
int* findAndDouble(int* arr, int len, int target) {
    int* result = new int(0);
    for (int i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
        if (arr[i] == target) {
            *result = arr[i] * 2;
            return result; // fine, caller is responsible for this one
        }
    }
    return nullptr; // BUG: "result" was already allocated and is now leaked
}
```

```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <vector>

// Better version: no manual new/delete at all. std::optional expresses
// "might not have a value" directly, with no heap allocation or ownership
// question at all for this case.
std::optional<int> findAndDouble(const std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    for (int x : arr) {
        if (x == target) {
            return x * 2;
        }
    }
    return std::nullopt;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> data{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    if (auto result = findAndDouble(data, 3)) {
        std::cout << "found, doubled: " << *result << "\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "not found\n";
    }

…
```

The original C-style enum. Its values live in the surrounding scope, not inside the enum's own namespace, which means they can collide with other names and, worse, implicitly convert to int without any cast.
```
#include <iostream>

enum Color { red, green, blue }; // red, green, blue are now names in the enclosing scope

int main() {
    Color c = red;
    int as_int = c; // implicit conversion to int, no cast needed, easy to do by accident
    std::cout << as_int << "\n";
}
```

C++11's enum class fixes both problems: values are scoped to the enum's own name (Color::red, not just red), and there's no implicit conversion to int, an explicit static_cast is required if you want the underlying integer.
```
#include <iostream>

enum class Color { red, green, blue };

int main() {
    Color c = Color::red;
    // int as_int = c; // compile error: no implicit conversion
    int as_int = static_cast<int>(c); // explicit, and visibly intentional
    std::cout << as_int << "\n";
}
```
Default to enum class in new code. The only reason to reach for the older unscoped enum is interfacing with an existing API that already uses one, or the rare case where you want the implicit int conversion (bit flags combined with |, for instance, though a scoped enum with explicit casts is usually still clearer).

Every enum has an underlying integer type, int by default for unscoped enums, and also int by default for scoped enums, though you can specify a smaller one explicitly if the value range doesn't need it, which can matter for memory layout in large arrays of enum values.
```
enum class StatusCode : unsigned char { ok = 0, not_found = 1, error = 2 };

int main() {
    static_assert(sizeof(StatusCode) == 1, "expected a 1-byte underlying type");
    return 0;
}
```

A struct bundles related fields together. When a struct has no constructors, no private/protected members, and no virtual functions (an "aggregate"), you can initialize all its members directly with brace initialization, in declaration order:
```
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

int main() {
    Point p{3, 4}; // aggregate initialization: x = 3, y = 4, in declaration order
    std::cout << p.x << ", " << p.y << "\n";
}
```

C++20 lets you name which member you're initializing, matching a feature C has had for a while. This makes initialization order-independent and self-documenting, especially valuable once a struct has more than two or three fields.
```
// godbolt-run -std=c++20
#include <iostream>

struct Config {
    int width;
    int height;
    bool fullscreen;
};

int main() {
    Config cfg{.width = 1920, .height = 1080, .fullscreen = true};
    std::cout << cfg.width << "x" << cfg.height << (cfg.fullscreen ? " fullscreen" : "") << "\n";
}
```

This surprises a lot of people coming from languages where struct and class mean different things: in C++, they compile to the exact same kind of type, with exactly one difference. Members and base classes default to public in a struct, and to private in a class. That's it. Everything else, constructors, methods, inheritance, virtual functions, works identically for both keywords.
```
struct S {
    int x; // public by default
};

class C {
    int x; // private by default
};

int main() {
    S s;
    s.x = 5; // fine, public

    C c;
    // c.x = 5; // compile error: x is private
    return 0;
}
```
In practice, most code uses struct for simple data-holding types with no invariants to protect (plain aggregates like the Point and Config above), and class for types with real behavior, private state, and constructors that enforce invariants. That's a convention, not a language rule, but it's a strong and nearly universal one, and deviating from it without a good reason will confuse anyone reading your code.

Try it yourself: convert an unscoped enum and add designated initializers›
Convert Direction below to a scoped enum class, fix the resulting compile errors at each use site, then add a Player struct with at least three fields and construct one using designated initializers.
```
#include <iostream>

enum Direction { north, south, east, west };

int main() {
    Direction d = north;
    int as_int = d;
    std::cout << as_int << "\n";
}
```

assert(condition) (from <cassert>) checks a condition and aborts the program immediately if it's false, but only in debug builds. Defining NDEBUG (which most build systems do automatically for release builds) strips asserts out entirely, so they cost nothing in production and should never be used for anything the program depends on at runtime.
```
#include <cassert>
#include <iostream>

double safe_divide(double a, double b) {
    assert(b != 0.0 && "safe_divide: divisor must not be zero");
    return a / b;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << safe_divide(10.0, 2.0) << "\n";
}
```
The && "message" trick above is a common idiom: a non-null string pointer is always truthy, so ANDing it into the condition doesn't change the logic, but the message shows up in the assertion failure output and makes debugging faster.

static_assert(condition, "message") checks a condition at compile time and fails the build (not the running program) if it's false. Since it's compile-time, the condition has to be something the compiler can evaluate without running your program, sizes of types, template parameters, constexpr values.
```
static_assert(sizeof(int) >= 4, "this code assumes int is at least 32 bits");

int main() {
    return 0;
}
```

std::optional<T> (from <optional>) wraps a value that may or may not be present, without needing a sentinel value like -1 or nullptr that could be confused with a legitimate result.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <string>

std::optional<int> parse_positive_int(const std::string& s) {
    if (s.empty()) return std::nullopt;
    int value = 0;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') return std::nullopt;
        value = value * 10 + (c - '0');
    }
    return value;
}

int main() {
    for (const auto& input : {"42", "abc", ""}) {
        if (auto result = parse_positive_int(input)) {
            std::cout << input << " -> " << *result << "\n";
        } else {
            std::cout << input << " -> not a valid positive integer\n";
        }
    }
}
```
Dereference an optional with * or -> once you've confirmed it holds a value (via the if check, or .has_value()). Dereferencing an empty optional is undefined behavior, exactly the kind of programmer error assert exists to catch during development.

This attribute marks a function's return value as something the caller shouldn't silently throw away. It's a small thing, but it directly prevents a real class of bugs: writing error-signaling functions that return a status and then forgetting to check it.
```
#include <iostream>

[[nodiscard]] bool try_parse(int& out, const char* text) {
    if (text == nullptr) return false;
    out = 0;
    for (const char* p = text; *p; ++p) {
        if (*p < '0' || *p > '9') return false;
        out = out * 10 + (*p - '0');
    }
    return true;
}

int main() {
    int value;
    try_parse(value, "123"); // compiler warns: ignoring [[nodiscard]] return value
    std::cout << value << "\n";
}
```
Try it yourself: replace a sentinel value with std::optional›
This function uses -1 to mean "not found", which silently breaks if -1 ever becomes a valid index in some future version of the code. Rewrite it to return std::optional<size_t> instead.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int find_index(const std::vector<int>& v, int target) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) {
        if (v[i] == target) return static_cast<int>(i);
    }
    return -1; // rewrite this function to use std::optional<size_t> instead
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{10, 20, 30};
    int idx = find_index(v, 20);
    std::cout << idx << "\n";
}
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

double divide(double a, double b) {
    if (b == 0.0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("division by zero");
    }
    return a / b;
}

int main() {
    try {
        std::cout << divide(10, 2) << "\n";
        std::cout << divide(5, 0) << "\n"; // throws
        std::cout << "never reached\n";
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
        std::cout << "caught: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }
}
```
throw raises an exception object. The runtime unwinds the call stack (destroying local objects along the way, in reverse order of construction) looking for a matching catch block. If none is found anywhere up the call stack, std::terminate is called and the program ends, so an uncaught exception is not a graceful failure.

As the stack unwinds looking for a handler, every local object that's gone out of scope gets destroyed normally, including any std::unique_ptr, std::vector, file handles wrapped in RAII types, and so on. This is exactly why RAII (resource acquisition is initialization, tying resource cleanup to an object's destructor) matters so much in C++: it's the mechanism that makes exception safety possible at all. A raw pointer with a manual delete somewhere later in the function will leak the moment an exception is thrown between the new and that delete; a unique_ptr never will, because its destructor runs regardless of how the function exits.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <stdexcept>

struct Resource {
    Resource() { std::cout << "acquired\n"; }
    ~Resource() { std::cout << "released\n"; }
};

void risky() {
    auto r = std::make_unique<Resource>();
    throw std::runtime_error("something went wrong");
    // r's destructor still runs during unwinding, even though we never reach here
}

int main() {
    try {
        risky();
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "caught: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }
}
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

struct Careful {
    Careful(bool shouldThrow) {
        std::cout << "Careful constructed\n";
        if (shouldThrow) throw std::runtime_error("boom");
    }
    ~Careful() { std::cout << "Careful destroyed\n"; }
};

struct Pair {
    Careful first;
    Careful second;
    Pair() : first(false), second(true) {} // second throws
};

int main() {
    try {
        Pair pair;
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "caught: " << e.what() << "\n";
        // notice: "first" WAS destroyed even though Pair's constructor never finished
    }
}
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <array>

int main() {
    std::array<int, 5> a{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    std::cout << a.size() << "\n";      // 5, known and queryable, unlike a C array
    std::cout << a.at(2) << "\n";       // 3, bounds-checked, throws std::out_of_range if wrong
    std::cout << a[2] << "\n";          // 3, unchecked, same as a C array's [] would be

    for (int x : a) std::cout << x << " "; // works with range-based for and <algorithm>
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```
std::array<T, N> stores its elements the same way a C array does, on the stack (or inline inside whatever object contains it), with zero extra runtime overhead. What you get for free is a real size(), bounds-checked access via .at(), and compatibility with the rest of the standard library's iterator-based interfaces.

A C-style array, when passed to a function or otherwise used in most expressions, silently decays into a pointer to its first element. The array's length is not part of that pointer. This is one of C++'s oldest and most persistent footguns, inherited straight from C.
```
#include <iostream>

void printSize(int arr[]) {
    // arr is actually just an "int*" here, size information is gone
    std::cout << sizeof(arr) << "\n"; // prints sizeof(int*): 8 (or 4), NOT the array's byte size
}

int main() {
    int numbers[10] = {0};
    std::cout << sizeof(numbers) << "\n"; // 40: sizeof(int) * 10, correct here
    printSize(numbers);                    // but decays to a pointer the moment it's passed
}
```
This is exactly why so much old C and C++ code passes a separate length parameter alongside every raw array parameter, void process(int* data, int length), there's no other way for the function to know how many elements it's looking at. std::array doesn't have this problem: passing one by reference keeps its real type, size() intact, and no separate length parameter needed.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <array>

template <std::size_t N>
void printSize(const std::array<int, N>& arr) {
    std::cout << arr.size() << "\n"; // the real element count, every time
}

int main() {
    std::array<int, 10> numbers{};
    printSize(numbers); // 10
}
```

Try it yourself: fix a decayed-array bug›
This function is supposed to compute the sum of a 5-element array, but it always gets the wrong answer for arrays other than size 5, silently, with no compiler error. Explain why, and fix it using std::array so the mistake becomes impossible to make.
c++
```
int sumFive(int arr[]) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]); ++i) { // BUG
        total += arr[i];
    }
    return total;
}
```
sizeof(arr) inside the function is sizeof(int*) (the decayed pointer), not the array's true byte size, so sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]) computes something like 8 / 4 = 2, not 5, regardless of the actual array passed in. The loop silently only sums the first 2 elements.
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <array>

int sumFive(const std::array<int, 5>& arr) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); ++i) { // arr.size() is always correct: no decay possible
        total += arr[i];
    }
    return total;
}

int main() {
    std::array<int, 5> nums{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::cout << sumFive(nums) << "\n"; // 15, correctly
}
```

Two functions can share a name if they differ in the number or types of their parameters. The compiler picks the best match at compile time based on the argument types at each call site, this is resolved statically, not at runtime.
```
#include <iostream>

void print(int x) { std::cout << "int: " << x << "\n"; }
void print(double x) { std::cout << "double: " << x << "\n"; }
void print(const char* x) { std::cout << "string: " << x << "\n"; }

int main() {
    print(5);
    print(3.14);
    print("hello");
}
```

The compiler ranks candidate overloads by how "exact" the match is: an exact type match beats a promotion (like char to int), which beats a standard conversion (like int to double), which beats a user-defined conversion. If two overloads are an equally good match for a given call, the call is ambiguous and fails to compile rather than picking one arbitrarily.
```
void f(int) {}
void f(double) {}

int main() {
    // f(5);      // fine, exact match on int
    // f(5L);     // ambiguous! long can convert to either int or double,
                  // and neither conversion is preferred over the other
    return 0;
}
```

A parameter can have a default value, letting callers omit it. Default arguments interact with overloading in a way worth knowing: they can create ambiguity with an otherwise-valid overload if the resulting call could match either one.
```
#include <iostream>

void greet(const std::string& name, const std::string& greeting = "Hello") {
    std::cout << greeting << ", " << name << "!\n";
}

int main() {
    greet("Ada");
    greet("Grace", "Hi");
}
```

Overloading still means writing out every version by hand. A function template lets the compiler generate the version it needs, for whatever type you call it with, from a single generic definition:
```
#include <iostream>

template<typename T>
T max_of(T a, T b) {
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << max_of(3, 7) << "\n";       // T deduced as int
    std::cout << max_of(3.5, 2.1) << "\n";   // T deduced as double
    std::cout << max_of('a', 'z') << "\n";   // T deduced as char
}
```
T here is a template parameter, a placeholder for a type the compiler fills in based on the arguments at each call site. This is called template argument deduction, and it's usually implicit: you don't write max_of<int>(3, 7), the compiler figures out T = int from the arguments themselves.

And it means heavy template use can noticeably increase compile times and binary size, since every distinct instantiation is separate generated code.
Try it yourself: turn three overloads into one template›
These three overloads do the same thing for three different types. Replace them with a single function template, then confirm all three call sites still work unchanged.
```
#include <iostream>

int square(int x) { return x * x; }
double square(double x) { return x * x; }
long square(long x) { return x * x; }

int main() {
    std::cout << square(4) << "\n";
    std::cout << square(2.5) << "\n";
    std::cout << square(100000L) << "\n";
}
```

C++, End to End
A function has a declaration (the signature: name, parameter types, return type) and a definition (the body). They can be the same statement, or two separate ones, and that separation is the entire reason multi-file C++ programs are possible.
```
#include <iostream>

// declaration (a "forward declaration"): tells the compiler this function
// exists and what it looks like, without providing the body yet
int add(int a, int b);

int main() {
    std::cout << add(2, 3) << "\n";  // legal: the compiler only needs the declaration here
}

// definition: the actual body, can come later in the same file or in a
// different translation unit entirely
int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}
```
The compiler only needs to know a function's signature to generate a call to it. It doesn't need the body until link time, when the linker has to find that body somewhere. That's the whole trick behind splitting a program across files.

Parameters are passed by value by default: the function gets its own copy, and changes inside the function don't touch the caller's variable. That's usually what you want for small types (int, double, bool) and usually wrong for anything larger, since it means an unnecessary copy. References (covered properly in a later chapter) fix that; for now, know that pass-by-value is the default and it's a real copy, not a nickname for the caller's variable.
```
#include <iostream>

void tryToModify(int x) {
    x = 100;  // only changes the local copy
}

int main() {
    int value = 5;
    tryToModify(value);
    std::cout << value << "\n";  // still 5
}
```
A function with no meaningful value to return uses void. A function declared to return something non-void must return something on every possible path, or the behavior of falling off the end is undefined (main is the one specific, documented exception to this rule).

A header file (.h or.hpp) exists to be #included by multiple.cpp files, and by convention it holds declarations, not definitions. Split a small program into three files to see why this matters:
c++
```
// math_utils.h
#ifndef MATH_UTILS_H
#define MATH_UTILS_H

int add(int a, int b);
int multiply(int a, int b);

#endif
```
c++
```
// math_utils.cpp
#include "math_utils.h"

int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int multiply(int a, int b) { return a * b; }
```
c++
```
// main.cpp
#include "math_utils.h"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << add(2, 3) << "\n";
    std::cout << multiply(2, 3) << "\n";
}
```
main.cpp never sees the definitions of add or multiply, only their declarations through the header. The compiler compiles main.cpp and math_utils.cpp completely independently into two object files; the linker is the only stage that needs both bodies to exist somewhere.
The one-definition rule (ODR) is the reason headers hold declarations and not definitions: a non-inline function or a non-template variable can have exactly one definition across the entire program. Put a function body in a header, include that header from two.cpp files, and you get two definitions of the same function at link time: a redefinition error. Declarations are exempt from this because they carry no code, just a promise.

```
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::int32_t a{};   // exactly 32 bits, guaranteed
    std::uint64_t b{};  // exactly 64 bits, unsigned, guaranteed
    std::cout << sizeof(a) << " " << sizeof(b) << "\n";
}
```
<cstdint> gives you std::int8_t through std::int64_t (and the unsigned versions) specifically so you never have to guess. Reach for these anywhere the exact width matters (file formats, network protocols, bit-packed structures); reach for plain int everywhere else, since it's the type the compiler and standard library are most optimized to work with.

sizeof(T) tells you how many bytes an object of type T occupies on the platform you're compiling for, right now, on this compiler. It's a compile-time operator, not a runtime function call, and its result can (and does) differ across platforms for the exact same source code.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "bool: "  << sizeof(bool)  << "\n";
    std::cout << "char: "  << sizeof(char)  << "\n";
    std::cout << "int: "   << sizeof(int)   << "\n";
    std::cout << "long: "  << sizeof(long)  << "\n";
    std::cout << "double: "<< sizeof(double)<< "\n";
}
```
Try it yourself: run that on Compiler Explorer with a few different compilers/targets from the dropdown (try an ARM target alongside the default x86-64 one). sizeof(long) is the one most likely to differ: 8 bytes on 64-bit Linux/macOS, 4 bytes on 64-bit Windows. That single inconsistency has broken real cross-platform code.

A signed integer type can represent negative numbers; an unsigned one cannot, and in exchange gets roughly double the positive range for the same bit width. Unsigned types wrap around on overflow instead of triggering undefined behavior (an actual, well-defined guarantee, unlike signed overflow), which sounds like a safety feature and is one of the most common sources of subtle bugs in C++.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    unsigned int a = 0;
    a = a - 1;                 // wraps around: no error, no warning by default
    std::cout << a << "\n";    // prints a huge number, not -1
}
```
The classic trap: looping backward with an unsigned index.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3};
    for (std::size_t i = v.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {  // i is unsigned: never goes negative
        std::cout << v[i] << "\n";
        // when i wraps past 0, it becomes a huge number, not -1,
        // so i >= 0 stays true forever and this reads out of bounds
    }
}
```

An implicit conversion happens automatically whenever a value of one type is used somewhere a different type is expected, no cast required. Some of these are perfectly safe (int to double, since every int value fits in a double). Some lose information silently.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    double d = 3.99;
    int i = d;               // implicit, narrowing, silently truncates to 3
    std::cout << i << "\n";

    int big = 70000;
    short s = big;           // implicit, narrowing, wraps to something nowhere near 70000
    std::cout << s << "\n";
}
```
Compare that with brace initialization from the first chapter: short s{big}; refuses to compile precisely because it's a narrowing conversion. Assignment (s = big;) allows it silently; initialization with braces does not. That asymmetry is a real, deliberate reason to reach for brace initialization everywhere you can.
An explicit conversion (a cast) says "I know this might lose information, do it anyway." Prefer static_cast over the C-style cast (T)value: it only allows conversions that make sense for the types involved, and it's visually greppable, which matters when you're hunting for every place a program deliberately throws away precision.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums{5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2};

    // instead of hand-writing a loop to find the max...
    int maxVal = nums[0];
    for (int n : nums) if (n > maxVal) maxVal = n;

    // ...reach for the algorithm that already says what you mean:
    int maxVal2 = *std::max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());

    std::cout << maxVal << " " << maxVal2 << "\n";
}
```
std::max_element isn't just shorter, it's more honest about intent: a reader sees the name and immediately knows what the code does, instead of having to trace through a hand-written loop to reverse-engineer that it's finding a maximum.

```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x;         // uninitialized: reading x right now is undefined behavior
    int y{};       // zero-initialized, always safe
    int z = 42;    // explicitly initialized

    std::cout << y << " " << z << "\n"; // never print x before assigning it something real
}
```
An uninitialized variable in C++ doesn't reliably contain zero. It contains whatever bits happened to already be sitting in that memory, which might look like a plausible value while testing and then be garbage the moment the stack layout shifts slightly. Prefer brace initialization ({}) as a habit; it also refuses narrowing conversions that would silently lose data, which is a nice bonus catch.

```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Edit this, then press the run arrow above.\n";
}
```
If you'd rather compile locally: any recent g++ or clang++ with -std=c++20 will run almost everything here. A handful of later examples reach for C++23 and say so explicitly.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Animal {
protected:
    std::string name;
public:
    Animal(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {
        std::cout << name << " (Animal) constructed\n";
    }
    ~Animal() {
        std::cout << name << " (Animal) destroyed\n";
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog(std::string n) : Animal(std::move(n)) {
        std::cout << name << " (Dog) constructed\n";
    }
    ~Dog() {
        std::cout << name << " (Dog) destroyed\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Dog d("Rex");
}
```
Notice the construction/destruction order in the output: base class constructor runs first, then derived. Destruction is the exact reverse: derived destructor first, then base. This makes sense once you think about it: a Dog can't finish constructing until the Animal part of it exists, and you shouldn't tear down the Animal part while the Dog part might still need it.

C++ allows a class to inherit from more than one base class. This is used far less than single inheritance, and for good reason: if two base classes both inherit from a common ancestor, a derived class inheriting from both ends up with two copies of that ancestor's data, the classic diamond problem.
```
#include <iostream>

struct Base { int value = 1; };
struct Left : Base {};
struct Right : Base {};
struct Diamond : Left, Right {}; // has TWO Base subobjects, ambiguous

int main() {
    Diamond d;
    // d.value; // error: ambiguous, is it Left::Base::value or Right::Base::value?
    d.Left::value = 5; // must disambiguate explicitly
    std::cout << d.Left::value << " " << d.Right::value << "\n"; // two separate values
}
```
C++ has virtual inheritance to solve this (a single shared Base subobject instead of two), but the honest advice is: avoid multiple inheritance of implementation entirely where you can. Multiple inheritance of pure interfaces (abstract classes with no data, covered in the next chapter) is much safer and common in real code; multiple inheritance of classes that hold state is where the trouble starts.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Animal {
protected:
    std::string name;
public:
    Animal(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) { std::cout << name << " Animal ctor\n"; }
    ~Animal() { std::cout << name << " Animal dtor\n"; }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog(std::string n) : Animal(std::move(n)) { std::cout << name << " Dog ctor\n"; }
    ~Dog() { std::cout << name << " Dog dtor\n"; }
};

class Puppy : public Dog {
public:
    Puppy(std::string n) : Dog(std::move(n)) { std::cout << name << " Puppy ctor\n"; }
    ~Puppy() { std::cout << name << " Puppy dtor\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Puppy p("Buddy");
}
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>

int main() {
    double pi = 3.14159265;
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2) << pi << "\n"; // 3.14
    std::cout << std::setw(10) << std::setfill('*') << 42 << "\n"; // ******   42, right-aligned
    std::cout << std::hex << 255 << "\n"; // ff
    std::cout << std::dec << 255 << "\n"; // back to decimal: 255
}
```
Manipulators like std::fixed and std::hex are sticky: they change the stream's state until you change it again. setw is the exception, it only applies to the very next thing written, which trips people up the first time they use it inside a loop and forget to repeat it.

std::cin >> someInt fails cleanly (sets a fail flag) if the input isn't a valid integer, but it does NOT throw or crash, and the leftover bad input stays in the stream's buffer waiting to break your very next read too, unless you clear the state and discard it.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    int value;
    std::cout << "enter a number: ";
    while (!(std::cin >> value)) {
        std::cout << "that wasn't a number, try again: ";
        std::cin.clear(); // clear the fail flag
        std::cin.ignore(std::numeric_limits<std::streamsize>::max(), '\n'); // discard the bad input
    }
    std::cout << "got: " << value << "\n";
}
```
If you skip the clear()/ignore() pair after a failed read, the stream stays in a failed state forever: every subsequent >> silently does nothing, and you get an infinite loop that never prompts again. This is one of the most common real bugs in beginner C++ input code.

std::stringstream lets you use the exact same >> and << operators you already know, but against an in-memory string instead of the console. It's the standard tool for splitting a line into fields:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string line = "42 3.14 hello";
    std::stringstream ss(line);

    int i;
    double d;
    std::string s;
    ss >> i >> d >> s;

    std::cout << "int: " << i << ", double: " << d << ", string: " << s << "\n";
}
```

std::ifstream and std::ofstream work like cin/cout once opened, since they're all built on the same iostream hierarchy. Always check whether the open succeeded before reading or writing; a missing file is a normal, expected failure mode, not an exceptional one.
```
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    {
        std::ofstream out("example.txt");
        if (!out) {
            std::cerr << "couldn't open file for writing\n";
            return 1;
        }
        out << "line one\n" << "line two\n";
    } // out closed automatically here, RAII again

    std::ifstream in("example.txt");
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(in, line)) {
        std::cout << "read: " << line << "\n";
    }
}
```
Try it yourself: parse comma-separated values›
Use a stringstream and std::getline with a custom delimiter to split a comma-separated line into individual fields.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::string csv = "apple,banana,cherry";
    std::stringstream ss(csv);
    std::string field;
    std::vector<std::string> fields;

    while (std::getline(ss, field, ',')) {
        fields.push_back(field);
    }

    for (const auto& f : fields) std::cout << "field: " << f << "\n";
}
```

A member variable belongs to each instance of the class. A member function is a function that has implicit access to a particular instance's member variables, without you needing to pass that instance in as an argument.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Point {
public:
    double x;
    double y;

    double distanceFromOrigin() const {
        return std::sqrt(x * x + y * y);
    }
};

int main() {
    Point p{3.0, 4.0};
    std::cout << p.distanceFromOrigin() << "\n"; // 5
}
```
Inside distanceFromOrigin, x and y refer to the member variables of whichever Point object the function was called on. There's no explicit parameter for that object because it's passed implicitly.

Every non-static member function secretly takes an extra parameter: a pointer to the object it was called on, named this. You almost never need to write this explicitly, but it explains a few things that otherwise look like magic: how the compiler knows which object's x you mean, and why a member function can return *this to support chaining.
```
#include <iostream>

class Counter {
    int count = 0;
public:
    Counter& increment() {
        ++this->count; // "this->count" and "count" mean the same thing here
        return *this;  // return a reference to the current object
    }
    int value() const { return count; }
};

int main() {
    Counter c;
    c.increment().increment().increment(); // chained because increment() returns Counter&
    std::cout << c.value() << "\n"; // 3
}
```

A constructor is a special member function that runs when an object is created. Its job is to establish the object's invariants, whatever must be true about its state for the rest of the class to work correctly. If a type has no sensible "default" state, don't give it a default constructor; make the caller supply what's needed.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Rectangle {
    double width;
    double height;
public:
    Rectangle(double w, double h) : width(w), height(h) {}

    double area() const { return width * height; }
};

int main() {
    Rectangle r(3.0, 4.0);
    std::cout << r.area() << "\n"; // 12
}
```
Member initializer lists
The : width(w), height(h) part is a member initializer list. It runs before the constructor body and directly initializes each member. This isn't just style. Two categories of members can only be set this way, never by assignment inside the body:

const members: once a const member is default-constructed (left uninitialized), you can never assign to it, so it must be initialized in the list.
Reference members: a reference must be bound to something at the moment it's created; there's no such thing as an unbound reference to assign to later.
```
#include <iostream>

class Named {
    const int id;
    std::string& logRef;
public:
    Named(int i, std::string& log) : id(i), logRef(log) {
        // id = i;      would not compile: can't assign to a const member
        // logRef = log; would not compile the same way for a reference member
    }
    int getId() const { return id; }
};

int main() {
    std::string log = "log";
    Named n(42, log);
    std::cout << n.getId() << "\n";
}
```
There's a second, subtler reason to prefer the initializer list even for ordinary members: members are always initialized in the order they're declared in the class, not the order they appear in the initializer list. If member B's initialization depends on member A already having a value, list order won't save you if the declaration order is wrong. Some compilers warn about this (-Wreorder), and it's worth keeping on.

Default and parameterized constructors, together
A class can have multiple constructors, overloaded like any other function. If you provide a parameterized constructor and still want a no-argument way to create the object, you have to write the default constructor explicitly. It's not there for free once you've added any other constructor.
```
#include <iostream>

class Vector2D {
    double x, y;
public:
    Vector2D() : x(0), y(0) {}                  // default constructor
    Vector2D(double x_, double y_) : x(x_), y(y_) {} // parameterized

    void print() const { std::cout << "(" << x << ", " << y << ")\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Vector2D origin;         // uses the default constructor
    Vector2D point(1.0, 2.0); // uses the parameterized one
    origin.print();
    point.print();
}
```

public, private, and protected control what code outside the class can touch. public members are part of the class's interface, anyone can use them. private members are implementation detail, only the class's own member functions (and friends, covered later) can touch them. protected is like private but also visible to derived classes; it matters once you get to inheritance.
Encapsulation isn't about hiding data for its own sake. It's about controlling the ways an object's state can change, so the class can guarantee its own invariants. If every member is public, any code anywhere can put the object into a state the class never intended, and there's no single place left to enforce correctness.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

class BankAccount {
    double balance;
public:
    BankAccount(double initial) : balance(initial) {
        if (initial < 0) throw std::invalid_argument("initial balance can't be negative");
    }

    void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount < 0) throw std::invalid_argument("can't deposit a negative amount");
        balance += amount;
    }

    double getBalance() const { return balance; }
};

int main() {
    BankAccount acc(100.0);
    acc.deposit(50.0);
    std::cout << acc.getBalance() << "\n"; // 150

    // acc.balance = -1000; would not compile: balance is private.
…
```

If balance were public, nothing would stop acc.balance = -1000; from running, and the class's promise that balance never goes negative through deposit would be meaningless, because there'd be a second door into the object that skips the check entirely.
Try it yourself: a class with a broken invariant›
The Temperature class below is supposed to only ever store a value in Celsius, and always report it correctly in both Celsius and Fahrenheit. Something's wrong. Find it, then fix it so the invariant holds no matter how the object is used.
```
#include <iostream>

class Temperature {
public:
    double celsius;

    double fahrenheit() const {
        return celsius * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0;
    }
};

int main() {
    Temperature t{20.0};
    std::cout << t.fahrenheit() << "\n"; // 68, looks fine so far

    t.celsius = -300; // physically impossible, but nothing stops this
    std::cout << t.fahrenheit() << "\n";
}
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{10, 20, 30};

    auto it = v.begin(); // an iterator pointing at the first element
    std::cout << *it << "\n"; // 10, dereference like a pointer
    ++it;                      // advance, like a pointer
    std::cout << *it << "\n"; // 20

    for (auto x = v.begin(); x != v.end(); ++x) {
        std::cout << *x << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n"; // 10 20 30
}
```
v.begin() and v.end() are the same idea [start, end) half-open ranges use everywhere in C++: begin() points at the first element, end() points one past the last one, and it's never dereferenced, it only exists as a stopping point to compare against.
Iterator categories, briefly
Not every container's iterator supports the same operations. std::vector's iterator supports random access (jump to any position in O(1), like it + 5). A std::forward_list's iterator can only move forward one step at a time. The standard groups these into categories, input, forward, bidirectional, random-access, and templates on <algorithm> are written in terms of the weakest category they need. std::sort requires random access (you can't efficiently sort something you can only walk forward through one element at a time), so it doesn't work on std::list, std::list has its own .sort() member function instead for exactly this reason.

The for (int x : v) syntax you've been using is sugar. The compiler rewrites it, roughly, into exactly the iterator loop from the first example: call v.begin() once, v.end() once, and loop comparing and advancing. Any type that provides begin()/end() (as members or as free functions found via argument-dependent lookup) works with range-based for automatically, which is how it works uniformly across every standard container and your own types too, if you write begin()/end() for them.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3};

    // This range-based for...
    for (int x : v) {
        std::cout << x << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";

    // ...is roughly equivalent to this:
    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
        int x = *it;
        std::cout << x << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

The standard library's algorithms operate on iterator ranges, not containers directly, that's precisely what lets one std::sort implementation work on a std::vector<int>, a std::vector<std::string>, or a raw array, as long as you hand it a valid [begin, end) pair with the right iterator category.
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <numeric>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{5, 3, 1, 4, 2};

    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << "\n"; // 1 2 3 4 5

    auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 3);
    if (it != v.end()) std::cout << "found 3 at index " << (it - v.begin()) << "\n";

    int total = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);
    std::cout << "sum = " << total << "\n"; // 15

    std::for_each(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { std::cout << x * x << " "; });
    std::cout << "\n"; // 1 4 9 16 25
}
```

c++
```
struct IntBox {
    int data[3];
    // Add begin()/end() here so "for (int x : box)" works
};
```
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>

struct IntBox {
    int data[3];

    int* begin() { return data; }
    int* end() { return data + 3; }
};

int main() {
    IntBox box{{10, 20, 30}};
    for (int x : box) {
        std::cout << x << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n"; // 10 20 30
}
```
A raw pointer already behaves exactly like a random-access iterator: *data dereferences, data + 3 advances (and data + 3 == end() is a valid comparison), so returning plain pointers from begin()/end() is a completely legitimate, common pattern for simple fixed-size types, not a shortcut or a hack.

```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    auto add = [](int a, int b) { return a + b; };
    std::cout << add(2, 3) << "\n"; // 5
}
```
Breaking down [](int a, int b) { return a + b; }:
[] is the capture list: what variables from the surrounding scope the lambda can see. Empty here, so it can only use its own parameters.
(int a, int b) is the parameter list, exactly like a regular function.
{ return a + b; } is the body, exactly like a regular function.
The return type is deduced automatically here. You can specify it explicitly with -> int after the parameter list if the deduction would be ambiguous or you just want to be explicit.

```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int multiplier = 3;

    auto byValue = [multiplier](int x) { return x * multiplier; };
    auto byRef    = [&multiplier](int x) { return x * multiplier; };

    multiplier = 10;

    std::cout << byValue(5) << "\n"; // 15: captured a COPY of multiplier when it was 3
    std::cout << byRef(5) << "\n";   // 50: captured a reference, sees the current value
}
```
[=] captures everything used in the body by value; [&] captures everything used by reference. Mixing is allowed too, [x, &y] captures x by value and y by reference specifically. Prefer naming exactly what you capture over the blanket [=]/[&] forms once a lambda gets non-trivial: it documents intent, and it avoids accidentally capturing something you didn't mean to.

A lambda that captures by reference is only as valid as the thing it references. If the lambda outlives the variable it captured (stored for later, returned from a function, handed off to run asynchronously), that reference dangles, and using it is undefined behavior, exactly like any other dangling reference.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <functional>

std::function<int()> makeBrokenCounter() {
    int count = 0; // local variable
    return [&count]() { return ++count; }; // captures "count" BY REFERENCE
    // "count" is destroyed when makeBrokenCounter() returns.
    // The returned lambda now holds a dangling reference.
}

int main() {
    auto counter = makeBrokenCounter();
    // std::cout << counter(); // undefined behavior: reads through a dangling reference
}
```
The fix is almost always to capture by value instead when the lambda's lifetime might outlast the enclosing scope:
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <functional>

std::function<int()> makeCounter() {
    int count = 0;
    return [count]() mutable { return ++count; }; // captures a COPY, owned by the lambda itself
}

int main() {
    auto counter = makeCounter();
    std::cout << counter() << "\n"; // 1
    std::cout << counter() << "\n"; // 2
    std::cout << counter() << "\n"; // 3
}
```

A function object is any class with operator() overloaded, letting an instance of it be called like a function. This is exactly what lambdas desugar to: every lambda is compiler-generated syntactic sugar for an anonymous class with an operator(), and (for reference captures) member references to whatever it captured.
```
#include <iostream>

class Multiplier {
    int factor;
public:
    Multiplier(int f) : factor(f) {}
    int operator()(int x) const { return x * factor; }
};

int main() {
    Multiplier triple(3);
    std::cout << triple(5) << "\n"; // 15, called just like a function

    // This is roughly what the compiler generates for a capturing lambda:
    auto tripleLambda = [factor = 3](int x) { return x * factor; };
    std::cout << tripleLambda(5) << "\n"; // 15, same idea
}
```
Writing a function object by hand today is rare, lambdas are shorter and clearer for almost every case a functor used to handle. It's worth knowing the pattern anyway: you'll see it in older code, and understanding that a lambda IS a function object (just one the compiler writes for you) removes a lot of the mystery around what capture means underneath.

std::function<Signature> is a type-erased wrapper that can hold any callable matching a given signature, a lambda, a function pointer, a function object, regardless of its actual underlying type. This is useful when you need to store heterogeneous callables (a vector of different callbacks, say) or pass a callable across an API boundary without templating that API on the callable's exact type.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <functional>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::function<int(int)>> operations;

    int factor = 2;
    operations.push_back([factor](int x) { return x * factor; });
    operations.push_back([](int x) { return x + 100; });

    for (auto& op : operations) {
        std::cout << op(5) << "\n"; // 10, then 105
    }
}
```

c++
```
std::function<bool(int)> makeThresholdCheck(int threshold) {
    return [&threshold](int x) { return x > threshold; }; // BUG: captures a reference to a parameter
}
```
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <functional>

std::function<bool(int)> makeThresholdCheck(int threshold) {
    return [threshold](int x) { return x > threshold; }; // capture by value: owns its own copy
}

int main() {
    auto isAdult = makeThresholdCheck(18);
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << isAdult(15) << "\n"; // false
    std::cout << isAdult(25) << "\n"; // true
}
```
threshold is a function parameter, a local variable that stops existing the moment makeThresholdCheck returns. Capturing it by reference produces exactly the same dangling-reference bug as the counter example above, just with a parameter instead of a local variable. The fix is identical: capture by value so the lambda owns its own independent copy.

A static data member belongs to the class itself, not to any one object. Every instance shares the same one. A static member function can be called without an object at all, and (because it isn't tied to an instance) has no this pointer, so it can only touch other static members.
```
#include <iostream>

class Widget {
    static int count; // declaration only; needs a definition outside the class
public:
    Widget() { ++count; }
    ~Widget() { --count; }
    static int activeCount() { return count; }
};

int Widget::count = 0; // the one required definition, usually in a .cpp file

int main() {
    {
        Widget a, b, c;
        std::cout << Widget::activeCount() << "\n"; // 3
    }
    std::cout << Widget::activeCount() << "\n"; // 0, all three destructed
}
```
A static data member needs exactly one definition somewhere (traditionally a.cpp file), separate from its declaration in the class. static inline int count = 0; inside the class (C++17 and later) lets you skip the separate definition entirely, and is the easier default now.

Marking a member function const promises it won't modify the object it's called on. This isn't just documentation. The compiler enforces it: a const member function can't assign to member variables, and it's the only kind of member function you can call on a const object or through a const reference.
```
#include <iostream>

class Box {
    double volume;
public:
    Box(double v) : volume(v) {}

    double getVolume() const { return volume; }     // read-only, correctly const
    void scale(double factor) { volume *= factor; }  // mutates, not const

    void describe(const Box& b) const {
        std::cout << b.getVolume() << "\n"; // fine: getVolume() is const
        // b.scale(2.0);                    would not compile: b is a const reference,
        //                                   scale() isn't a const member function
    }
};

int main() {
    Box b(10.0);
    b.describe(b);
}
```

A friend declaration gives an outside function or class access to your private members, as if it were a member itself. It breaks encapsulation on purpose, for one specific relationship, and should be used sparingly: reach for it only when two things are so tightly coupled that a clean public interface between them would be more awkward than the access hole.
```
#include <iostream>

class Matrix; // forward declaration

class Vector3 {
    double data[3];
public:
    Vector3(double x, double y, double z) : data{x, y, z} {}
    friend class Matrix; // Matrix can reach into Vector3's private data
    void print() const { std::cout << data[0] << ", " << data[1] << ", " << data[2] << "\n"; }
};

class Matrix {
public:
    void zeroOut(Vector3& v) {
        v.data[0] = v.data[1] = v.data[2] = 0.0; // allowed only because of the friend declaration
    }
};

int main() {
    Vector3 v(1, 2, 3);
    Matrix m;
    m.zeroOut(v);
    v.print(); // 0, 0, 0
}
```

A constructor can call another constructor of the same class in its initializer list, instead of duplicating setup logic. This is delegation, and it keeps constructors that differ only by defaults from repeating themselves.
```
#include <iostream>

class Circle {
    double radius;
    std::string label;
public:
    Circle(double r, std::string l) : radius(r), label(std::move(l)) {}
    Circle(double r) : Circle(r, "unnamed") {} // delegates to the constructor above
    Circle() : Circle(1.0) {}                   // delegates again

    void print() const { std::cout << label << ": r=" << radius << "\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Circle a(5.0, "big");
    Circle b(2.0);
    Circle c;
    a.print();
    b.print();
    c.print();
}
```

You can give a member variable a default value right where it's declared. Any constructor that doesn't explicitly initialize that member in its own list picks up the default automatically. This removes a whole class of "forgot to initialize a field in one of three constructors" bugs.
```
#include <iostream>

class Config {
    int timeoutSeconds = 30;   // default member initializer
    bool verbose = false;

public:
    Config() = default; // uses both defaults
    Config(int timeout) : timeoutSeconds(timeout) {} // overrides just one

    void print() const {
        std::cout << timeoutSeconds << ", " << verbose << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Config a;
    Config b(60);
    a.print(); // 30, 0
    b.print(); // 60, 0
}
```

If a class manages a resource directly (raw memory via new, a file handle, a socket), the compiler-generated copy constructor, copy assignment operator, and destructor will do the wrong thing: they copy the pointer, not what it points to. Two objects end up owning the same resource, and when both destructors run, you get a double free.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>

class BadBuffer {
    char* data;
public:
    BadBuffer(const char* text) {
        data = new char[std::strlen(text) + 1];
        std::strcpy(data, text);
    }
    ~BadBuffer() { delete[] data; }
    // No copy constructor or copy assignment written: the compiler generates ones
    // that just copy the "data" pointer, not the buffer it points to.
};

int main() {
    BadBuffer a("hello");
    BadBuffer b = a; // compiler-generated copy: b.data now points at the SAME memory as a.data
    // When main() ends, both a and b are destructed, and both destructors call
    // delete[] on the same pointer. That's a double free: undefined behavior.
}
```

Don't run the example above for real, it's a genuine double free. The point is to see why the bug exists, not to trigger it.
The fix is either the rule of three (write a copy constructor, copy assignment operator, and destructor together, since needing one strongly implies needing the other two) or, better, the rule of zero: don't manage the raw resource yourself at all. Wrap it in a type that already does this correctly, like std::string, std::vector, or a smart pointer, and let your class's compiler-generated special members work by simply calling that member's own correct versions.
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class GoodBuffer {
    std::string data; // owns its own memory correctly; no manual new/delete anywhere
public:
    GoodBuffer(const char* text) : data(text) {}
    void print() const { std::cout << data << "\n"; }
    // No copy constructor, copy assignment, or destructor written at all.
    // The compiler-generated ones just copy/destroy the std::string member,
    // which already does the right thing.
};

int main() {
    GoodBuffer a("hello");
    GoodBuffer b = a; // a real, independent copy of the string, no shared ownership
    b.print();
    a.print();
}
```

Rule of five extends rule of three to also cover move constructor and move assignment, which matter once you care about avoiding unnecessary copies (the dedicated move semantics chapter elsewhere in this course covers that in depth). The practical takeaway is the same either way: reach for rule of zero by default, and only write the special members yourself when your class is the one place directly responsible for owning a raw resource.
Try it yourself: spot the missing rule-of-three piece›
This class manages a raw array itself, and writes a destructor and copy constructor, but is still broken. Find the missing piece, and explain what goes wrong without it.
c++
```
class IntArray {
    int* data;
    int size;
public:
    IntArray(int n) : size(n) { data = new int[n]{}; }
    ~IntArray() { delete[] data; }

    IntArray(const IntArray& other) : size(other.size) {
        data = new int[size];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) data[i] = other.data[i];
    }
    // No copy assignment operator (operator=) written.
};
```

T&& is an rvalue reference: a reference that only binds to rvalues (temporaries). It's what lets you write a function overload that specifically handles "this argument is disposable":
```
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

void greet(const std::string& name) {
    std::cout << "copying: " << name << "\n";
}

void greet(std::string&& name) {
    std::cout << "moving: " << name << "\n";
}

int main() {
    std::string s = "Ada";
    greet(s);            // lvalue, binds to const std::string&
    greet(std::string("Grace")); // rvalue (temporary), binds to std::string&&
    greet(std::move(s));         // s is now treated as an rvalue
}
```

A move constructor takes ownership of another object's resources instead of copying them, then leaves the source in a safe, empty state. For a class that owns a heap buffer, that usually means: steal the pointer, null out the source's pointer so its destructor doesn't free memory you now own.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>

class Buffer {
    char* data;
    size_t size;
public:
    Buffer(size_t n) : data(new char[n]), size(n) {
        std::cout << "allocated " << n << " bytes\n";
    }

    // Move constructor: steal the pointer, don't copy the bytes.
    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : data(other.data), size(other.size) {
        other.data = nullptr;
        other.size = 0;
        std::cout << "moved (no allocation)\n";
    }

    // Copy constructor: actually duplicate the bytes.
    Buffer(const Buffer& other) : data(new char[other.size]), size(other.size) {
        std::memcpy(data, other.data, size);
        std::cout << "copied " << size << " bytes\n";
    }
…
```

A std::unique_ptr<T> owns exactly one object and destroys it when the pointer itself is destroyed. It cannot be copied (there can only ever be one owner), only moved. If you find yourself writing new without immediately wrapping the result in a smart pointer, stop and ask why.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

struct Widget {
    Widget() { std::cout << "Widget built\n"; }
    ~Widget() { std::cout << "Widget destroyed\n"; }
};

void takeOwnership(std::unique_ptr<Widget> w) {
    std::cout << "using widget\n";
} // w destroyed here when the function returns

int main() {
    auto w = std::make_unique<Widget>();
    takeOwnership(std::move(w)); // ownership transferred, w is now null
    std::cout << "w is now " << (w ? "still owns something" : "empty") << "\n";
}
```
Always prefer std::make_unique<T>(args...) over new T(args...) wrapped by hand. It's exception-safe (no window where the raw pointer exists but hasn't been wrapped yet) and it's one less place to typo a mismatched delete.

A std::shared_ptr<T> allows multiple owners; the object is destroyed when the last shared_ptr pointing to it goes away. That flexibility isn't free: every shared_ptr carries a control block with an atomic reference count, copying one means an atomic increment, and destroying one means an atomic decrement plus a check. In a hot path, that overhead is real and measurable.
Reach for shared_ptr only when ownership is shared and unclear who outlives whom. If there's one clear owner, unique_ptr is both cheaper and more honest about the design.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

int main() {
    auto a = std::make_shared<int>(42);
    std::cout << "use_count: " << a.use_count() << "\n"; // 1
    {
        auto b = a; // shared ownership, ref count goes up
        std::cout << "use_count: " << a.use_count() << "\n"; // 2
    } // b destroyed, ref count goes back down
    std::cout << "use_count: " << a.use_count() << "\n"; // 1
}
```

Two objects holding shared_ptrs to each other will never reach a reference count of zero: a cycle. std::weak_ptr observes an object managed by shared_ptr without contributing to its reference count, which breaks the cycle. You can't dereference a weak_ptr directly; you call.lock() to get a temporary shared_ptr, which is null if the object's already gone.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

struct Node {
    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;
    std::weak_ptr<Node> prev; // weak: doesn't keep prev alive artificially
    ~Node() { std::cout << "Node destroyed\n"; }
};

int main() {
    auto a = std::make_shared<Node>();
    auto b = std::make_shared<Node>();
    a->next = b;
    b->prev = a; // if this were shared_ptr instead of weak_ptr, neither would ever be destroyed
}
```

Raw pointers aren't banned, they're just no longer the default for ownership. A raw T* or a reference is exactly right when you're observing an object you don't own and whose lifetime you know outlives your use of it: a function parameter that just reads or modifies something the caller owns, for instance. The rule: raw pointers for non-owning access, smart pointers for ownership.
Try it yourself: fix a use-after-move bug›
The function below moves data out of a Config object, then tries to read from it afterward. Run it, watch what happens (it may print an empty string, or it may not crash at all, which is exactly the danger of "valid but unspecified"), then fix it by not reading from the moved-from object.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

struct Config {
    std::string name;
};

void process(Config cfg) {
    std::string stolen = std::move(cfg.name);
    std::cout << "stolen: " << stolen << "\n";
    std::cout << "cfg.name is now: '" << cfg.name << "'\n"; // reading a moved-from object
}

int main() {
    Config c{"production-db"};
    process(c);
}
```

Composition means an object owns another object outright: the contained object is created when the container is, destroyed when the container is, and has no independent existence. A Car has an Engine; when the Car is destroyed, its Engine goes with it. The clearest signal in code: the member is a value (or a unique_ptr), not a pointer or reference to something that might outlive or be shared.
```
#include <iostream>

class Engine {
public:
    Engine() { std::cout << "Engine built\n"; }
    ~Engine() { std::cout << "Engine destroyed\n"; }
};

class Car {
    Engine engine; // composition: Car owns Engine outright
public:
    Car() { std::cout << "Car built\n"; }
    ~Car() { std::cout << "Car destroyed\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Car c; // building/destroying the Car automatically builds/destroys its Engine
}
```

Aggregation means one object has-a reference to another, but the two don't share a lifetime. A Department has Employees, but an Employee exists independently of any one Department (they can transfer, or the object modeling the department can be deleted without deleting the person). The signal in code: the member is a pointer or reference to something owned elsewhere, often a shared_ptr if shared, or a raw observing pointer if the aggregating object doesn't own it.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

class Employee {
    std::string name;
public:
    Employee(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {}
    const std::string& getName() const { return name; }
};

class Department {
    std::string name;
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Employee>> members; // aggregation: doesn't own them exclusively
public:
    Department(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {}
    void add(std::shared_ptr<Employee> e) { members.push_back(std::move(e)); }
};

int main() {
    auto alice = std::make_shared<Employee>("Alice");
    Department eng("Engineering");
    eng.add(alice); // Department knows about alice, doesn't own her exclusively
…
```

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

class Song {
    std::string title;
public:
    Song(std::string t) : title(std::move(t)) {}
    const std::string& getTitle() const { return title; }
};

class Playlist {
    std::vector<Song> songs; // composition: Playlist has-a collection of Songs
public:
    void add(Song s) { songs.push_back(std::move(s)); }
    void printAll() const {
        for (const auto& s : songs) std::cout << s.getTitle() << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Playlist p;
    p.add(Song("Clair de Lune"));
    p.add(Song("Gymnopedie No. 1"));
    p.printAll();
}
```

Some operators can be overloaded either as a member function or a free (non-member) function. Others have a hard requirement. operator<< for stream output is a classic case where it must be a free function: the left-hand operand is std::ostream&, not your type, and a member function's implicit left operand is always the object it's called on, so a member operator<< would need to be a member of std::ostream itself, which you don't own and can't modify.
```
#include <iostream>

class Point {
    double x, y;
public:
    Point(double x_, double y_) : x(x_), y(y_) {}

    // free function, declared as a friend so it can reach x and y directly
    friend std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Point& p) {
        return os << "(" << p.x << ", " << p.y << ")";
    }
};

int main() {
    Point p(3.0, 4.0);
    std::cout << p << "\n"; // (3, 4)
}
```
A general rule of thumb: if the left-hand operand should always be your type (like a + b where a is your class), a member function overload reads naturally, since it's called as a.operator+(b) under the hood. If the left-hand operand is something you don't own, or you want implicit conversions to apply symmetrically to both sides (so 2 + myNumber and myNumber + 2 both work, not just one direction), a free function is the right shape.

```
#include <iostream>

class Fraction {
    int num, den;
public:
    Fraction(int n, int d) : num(n), den(d) {}

    bool operator==(const Fraction& other) const {
        return num * other.den == other.num * den; // cross-multiply, avoids a division
    }
    bool operator!=(const Fraction& other) const {
        return !(*this == other);
    }
};

int main() {
    Fraction half(1, 2);
    Fraction alsoHalf(2, 4);
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << (half == alsoHalf) << "\n"; // true
}
```
Before C++20, you had to write every comparison operator (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=) by hand, even though most of them can be derived mechanically from just one or two of them. C++20's spaceship operator, <=>, lets the compiler generate the rest for you from a single three-way comparison:
```
// godbolt-run -std=c++20
#include <iostream>
#include <compare>

class Version {
    int major, minor;
public:
    Version(int maj, int min) : major(maj), minor(min) {}

    auto operator<=>(const Version&) const = default; // compares major, then minor, in order
    bool operator==(const Version&) const = default;
};

int main() {
    Version a(1, 5);
    Version b(1, 8);

    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << (a < b) << "\n";  // true, derived automatically from operator<=>
    std::cout << (a == b) << "\n"; // false
}
```

If you overload +, overload += too, and make sure they agree: a += b should do the same thing as a = a + b, just without the extra temporary and copy. Users of your type will reasonably assume this consistency holds, the way it does for every built-in numeric type, and breaking it (having + and += do subtly different things) is exactly the kind of surprising behavior operator overloading should avoid.
```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>

class Vector2D {
    double x, y;
public:
    Vector2D(double x_, double y_) : x(x_), y(y_) {}

    Vector2D& operator+=(const Vector2D& other) {
        x += other.x;
        y += other.y;
        return *this;
    }

    // Implemented in terms of operator+=, guaranteeing they agree by construction
    friend Vector2D operator+(Vector2D lhs, const Vector2D& rhs) {
        lhs += rhs;
        return lhs;
    }

    void print() const { std::cout << "(" << x << ", " << y << ")\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Vector2D a(1, 2);
    Vector2D b(3, 4);

    Vector2D c = a + b;
    c.print(); // (4, 6)

    a += b;
…
```

Nothing stops you from overloading + on a Logger type to mean "write two log messages", or overloading [] to run arbitrary unrelated logic instead of indexing. The compiler will happily let you. Readers of your code won't expect it, though, because every other type in the language uses + for addition-like combination and [] for indexing-like access. If an operation doesn't map naturally onto an existing operator's obvious meaning, give it a named member function instead. log.combine(other) is clearer than a + that does something a reader would never guess.
Try it yourself: implement operator+= and derive operator+ from it›
Given this Money class with only a constructor and a getter, add operator+= and derive operator+ from it, following the same pattern as the Vector2D example above, so the two stay consistent by construction rather than by careful duplication.
c++
```
class Money {
    long cents; // store as integer cents to avoid floating-point rounding issues
public:
    Money(long c) : cents(c) {}
    long getCents() const { return cents; }
    // Add operator+= and operator+ here
};
```

```
// godbolt-run
#include <iostream>

class Money {
    long cents;
public:
    Money(long c) : cents(c) {}
    long getCents() const { return cents; }

    Money& operator+=(const Money& other) {
        cents += other.cents;
        return *this;
    }

    friend Money operator+(Money lhs, const Money& rhs) {
        lhs += rhs;
        return lhs;
    }
};

int main() {
    Money a(150);  // $1.50
    Money b(275);  // $2.75
    Money c = a + b;
    std::cout << c.getCents() << "\n"; // 425

    a += b;
    std::cout << a.getCents() << "\n"; // 425, consistent with "c" above
}
```

c++
```
==  !=  <  >  <=  >=    // relational: produce bool
&&  ||  !               // logical AND, OR, NOT
```
```
#include <iostream>

bool isEven(int n) {
    std::cout << "checking " << n << "\n";
    return n % 2 == 0;
}

int main() {
    // short-circuit evaluation: the right side of && is never evaluated
    // if the left side is already false (same idea for || when the left is true)
    if (false && isEven(4)) {
        std::cout << "unreachable\n";
    }
    std::cout << "isEven(4) was never even called above\n";
}
```
Short-circuiting isn't just an optimization detail, real code depends on it being guaranteed. `ptr != nullptr && ptr->value > 0` is only safe because the language guarantees the right side never runs if the pointer is null; if it weren't guaranteed, that pattern would be undefined behavior half the time.

c++
```
&   // AND
|   // OR
^   // XOR
~   // NOT (unary, flips every bit)
<<  // left shift
>>  // right shift
```
These operate on the actual bit pattern of an integer, and this course has a full dedicated chapter on bit manipulation, since it's its own topic (flags, masks, packing multiple values into one integer). The one thing worth flagging immediately here: & and | are NOT shorthand for && and ||. Using a single & where you meant && still compiles, since it's a perfectly legal (different) operator, and the resulting bug can be very hard to spot by eye.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    bool a = true;
    bool b = false;
    std::cout << (a && b) << "\n";  // 0: real logical AND with short-circuiting
    std::cout << (a &  b) << "\n";  // 0 here too, coincidentally, but this is bitwise AND, not logical
    // with different values these can genuinely disagree; never use & where you mean &&
}
```

Precedence decides which operator binds tighter when two appear next to each other without parentheses; associativity decides the order when several operators of the same precedence appear in a row. C++'s precedence table has more than fifteen levels, and nobody has it fully memorized. The practical answer is: when in doubt, parenthesize. It costs nothing and removes the ambiguity for both the compiler and the next person reading the code.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int result = 2 + 3 * 4;        // 14, not 20: * binds tighter than +
    int clearer = 2 + (3 * 4);     // also 14, but the intent is now visible on sight
    std::cout << result << " " << clearer << "\n";
}
```

The comma operator evaluates its left operand, discards the result, then evaluates and returns its right operand. It shows up almost exclusively in the increment/update clause of a for loop, and almost anywhere else it's a sign the code should be two statements instead of one expression.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    for (int i = 0, j = 10; i < 5; i++, j--) {
        std::cout << i << " " << j << "\n";
    }
}
```
Careful: a comma inside function-call parentheses is argument separation, not the comma operator. `f(a, b)` calls f with two arguments; it never invokes the comma operator at all. The comma operator only applies where a single expression is expected and a literal comma appears inside it, which in practice is rare outside that for-loop pattern.

c++
```
condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse
```
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 7;
    int b = 3;
    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
    std::cout << max << "\n";
}
```
The ternary operator is an expression, not a statement, which is exactly why it can appear inside another expression (as an initializer, as a function argument) where an if/else block flatly cannot. That's its actual justification, not just "shorter than if/else." Nesting more than one ternary in a single expression reliably makes code harder to read than the equivalent if/else chain would have been; resist the urge.
Try it yourself: predict the output of `10 - 2 - 3` and `2 + 3 == 5 && 1` on paper first (subtraction and equality both have associativity/precedence rules worth confirming you know), then run them and check. The second one is a genuine trap: == binds tighter than &&, so it parses as `(2 + 3 == 5) && 1`, not something weirder.

A pointer is a variable that holds a memory address. &x gives you the address of x (the address-of operator), and *p accesses the value a pointer points to (the dereference operator). The same * symbol also declares a pointer type, which is a confusing overload of syntax worth just memorizing: int* p declares, *p dereferences.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x = 42;
    int* p = &x;

    std::cout << "x = " << x << "\n";
    std::cout << "address of x = " << p << "\n";
    std::cout << "value at that address = " << *p << "\n";

    *p = 100; // modifies x through the pointer
    std::cout << "x is now " << x << "\n";
}
```

A pointer that doesn't point at a valid object should be set to nullptr (C++11 and later; older code used the literal 0 or the NULL macro, both of which are worse because they can be confused with an actual integer zero in overload resolution). Dereferencing a null pointer is undefined behavior, typically a crash, but not guaranteed to be.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int* p = nullptr;
    if (p != nullptr) {
        std::cout << *p << "\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "p doesn't point at anything\n";
    }
}
```

A reference is an alias for an existing object, not a separate object that holds an address. Once bound, a reference can't be rebound to refer to something else, and it must be initialized at the point of declaration, there's no such thing as a null reference or an uninitialized one (in well-defined code).
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int& ref = x; // ref IS x now, not a pointer to it

    ref = 20; // this changes x directly
    std::cout << x << "\n"; // 20

    int y = 99;
    ref = y; // this assigns y's VALUE into x (through ref); it does NOT rebind ref to y
    std::cout << x << " " << y << "\n"; // 99 99
}
```
That last line is the single most common reference gotcha: ref = y; looks like it might make ref refer to y instead, but references can't be rebound after initialization. It's always an assignment through the reference to whatever it already refers to.

```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void by_value(std::string s) { s += "!"; }         // copies the whole string, caller unaffected
void by_ref(std::string& s) { s += "!"; }           // modifies the caller's string directly
void by_const_ref(const std::string& s) { /* read only, no copy */ }
void by_pointer(std::string* s) { if (s) *s += "!"; } // like by_ref, but caller must pass &variable, and null is a valid "no target" state

int main() {
    std::string greeting = "hi";
    by_value(greeting);
    std::cout << greeting << "\n"; // still "hi", by_value only changed its own copy

    by_ref(greeting);
    std::cout << greeting << "\n"; // "hi!", changed through the reference
}
```
By value when the function needs its own independent copy, or the type is small and cheap to copy (like int).
By const reference for read-only access to anything larger than a couple of machine words, avoids a copy with no risk of the callee modifying the caller's object.

Both references and pointers can outlive the object they refer to, at which point using them is undefined behavior. The classic case: returning a reference or pointer to a local variable, which is destroyed the moment the function returns.
```
#include <iostream>

int& dangling_ref() {
    int local = 42;
    return local; // BUG: local is destroyed when the function returns
}

int main() {
    int& r = dangling_ref(); // r now refers to a destroyed object
    std::cout << r << "\n";  // undefined behavior, may print garbage, may crash, may "work"
}
```
Most compilers will warn about this specific pattern (returning a reference to a local) with a plain "reference to local variable returned" warning. Always build with warnings on and treat them as errors during development; this is exactly the class of bug they exist to catch.
Try it yourself: spot and fix the dangling pointer›
This function has the same bug as the example above, but with a pointer instead of a reference. Find it, understand why it's wrong, and fix it (one reasonable fix: return by value instead, letting the caller own a real copy).

```
#include <iostream>

int* make_answer() {
    int answer = 42;
    return &answer; // find the bug
}

int main() {
    int* p = make_answer();
    std::cout << *p << "\n";
}
```

A name declared inside a block ({ }) has block (local) scope: visible from its declaration to the end of that enclosing block, and invisible outside it. A name declared at file scope, outside any function, has global scope: visible from its declaration to the end of the file (and beyond, if declared extern and linked to from elsewhere, see linkage below).
```
#include <iostream>

int global = 100;  // global scope

void f() {
    int local = 5;  // block scope: only visible inside f
    std::cout << local << " " << global << "\n";
}

int main() {
    f();
    // std::cout << local;  // error: local isn't visible here
    std::cout << global << "\n";  // fine: global scope reaches main too
}
```
Nested blocks can shadow an outer name: a local variable with the same name as a global (or an outer local) temporarily hides it for the rest of that inner block. This compiles, and most compilers warn about it with -Wshadow, precisely because it's a frequent source of "why did that use the wrong value" bugs.

Automatic duration: the default for local variables. Created when execution reaches the declaration, destroyed when the enclosing block ends. This is what makes a local variable's address unsafe to return or store past the function's lifetime.
Static duration: created once, before main runs, and destroyed once, after main returns. Global variables always have this. A local variable can opt into it with the static keyword, in which case it keeps its value between calls instead of being recreated each time.
Dynamic duration: created explicitly with new, destroyed explicitly with delete (or, in modern C++, managed automatically by a smart pointer, covered in a much later chapter). This is the only duration where you, not the compiler, are responsible for ending the object's life.
```
#include <iostream>

void counter() {
    static int calls = 0;  // initialized once, ever; persists across calls
    calls++;
    std::cout << "called " << calls << " time(s)\n";
}

int main() {
    counter();
    counter();
    counter();  // prints 1, 2, 3, not 1, 1, 1
}
```

Returning a reference or pointer to a local variable is a classic bug directly caused by mixing up scope and storage duration: the name goes out of scope at the closing brace, and the automatic-duration object it referred to is destroyed at that exact same point, so the returned reference/pointer is left dangling.
```
#include <iostream>

int& brokenFunction() {
    int local = 42;
    return local;  // compiles with a warning; the returned reference dangles immediately
}

int main() {
    int& r = brokenFunction();
    std::cout << r << "\n";  // undefined behavior: reads a destroyed object's old memory
}
```
Try it yourself: run the dangling-reference example above and read the actual compiler warning it produces (most compilers do catch this specific case). Then fix it by returning by value instead of by reference, and notice the warning disappears because a returned value is copied out before the local is destroyed.

A namespace groups related names under a common prefix, which is the language's actual mechanism for avoiding name collisions across a large program or between separate libraries ("my_math::add" and "their_math::add" can coexist without conflict, where two bare global "add" functions could not).
```
#include <iostream>

namespace geometry {
    double pi = 3.14159;

    double circleArea(double radius) {
        return pi * radius * radius;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::cout << geometry::circleArea(2.0) << "\n";
}
```
Namespaces can nest (geometry::shapes::circleArea), and can be reopened across multiple files, letting a single logical namespace span an entire library. An anonymous namespace (namespace {... }, no name) gives everything inside it internal linkage automatically, and in modern C++ it's generally preferred over scattering static in front of individual declarations, since it applies uniformly to everything inside without repetition.

```
#include <iostream>

namespace {
    int helper() { return 42; }  // internal linkage: private to this file, same effect as
}                                  // marking it static individually, but scales to many names

int main() {
    std::cout << helper() << "\n";
}
```

Global variables with static duration are initialized before main runs, but the standard only guarantees a specific order for globals within the same translation unit (in declaration order). Across different translation units, the order is unspecified. If one global's initializer depends on another global defined in a different.cpp file, there's a real chance it runs before that other global has been initialized, silently reading a zero-initialized (or otherwise not-yet-set) value.
c++
```
// file_a.cpp
int a = 10;
```
c++
```
// file_b.cpp
extern int a;
int b = a * 2;  // UNDEFINED whether a is 10 or 0 here, depending on link order,
                // which depends on your build system, not your source code
```
This is exactly the kind of bug that works fine for months and then breaks the moment someone reorders source files in a build script or switches compilers. The practical fix, when a global must depend on another global's value, is to wrap it in a function returning a local static instead: a local static is guaranteed to be initialized the first time control passes through its declaration, which sidesteps the whole cross-file ordering problem.

```
#include <iostream>

int& getA() {
    static int a = 10;  // initialized on first call, guaranteed, regardless of link order
    return a;
}

int b = getA() * 2;  // safe: calling getA() forces a's initialization first,
                      // sidestepping the cross-file ordering problem entirely

int main() {
    std::cout << b << "\n";
}
```
Try it yourself: this one is worth reading rather than running, since the bug it describes is nondeterministic by nature. Write down, in your own words, why a local static sidesteps the cross-file ordering problem that a plain global doesn't. If you can explain why, you understand the actual mechanism, not just the workaround.

```
#include <iostream>

template <typename T>
class Box {
    T value;
public:
    Box(T v) : value(v) {}
    T get() const { return value; }
    void set(T v) { value = v; }
};

int main() {
    Box<int> intBox(42);
    Box<std::string> stringBox("hello");
    std::cout << intBox.get() << " " << stringBox.get() << "\n";
}
```
Box<int> and Box<std::string> are two entirely different, independently compiled classes as far as the compiler is concerned, generated from the same template. This is why templates are described as compile-time polymorphism: there's no runtime dispatch overhead like virtual functions have, because there's no ambiguity left to resolve by the time the program runs, every use site already knows exactly which concrete type it's dealing with.

Sometimes the generic implementation is wrong (or just suboptimal) for a specific type. Full specialization lets you provide a completely separate implementation for one exact type:
```
#include <iostream>

template <typename T>
class Printer {
public:
    static void print(const T& value) { std::cout << value << "\n"; }
};

// full specialization: bool prints as "true"/"false" instead of 1/0
template <>
class Printer<bool> {
public:
    static void print(const bool& value) { std::cout << (value ? "true" : "false") << "\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Printer<int>::print(42);
    Printer<bool>::print(true); // uses the specialized version
}
```
Partial specialization does the same thing but for a subset of a multi-parameter template (for example, specializing a Pair<T, U> template just for when U is a pointer type), rather than one exact concrete type.

A variadic template accepts any number of template arguments, using a parameter pack (Args...). This is how things like std::make_unique and std::tuple are implemented under the hood.
```
#include <iostream>

template <typename... Args>
void printAll(Args... args) {
    ((std::cout << args << " "), ...); // C++17 fold expression
    std::cout << "\n";
}

int main() {
    printAll(1, "two", 3.0, 'f');
}
```
That ((std::cout << args << " "),...) is a fold expression (C++17): it expands the parameter pack, applying the comma operator across every argument, which is a compact way to "do this for every argument in the pack" without writing recursive template unpacking by hand, which is how this was done before C++17 and was considerably uglier.

Before C++20, constraining a template to only accept certain kinds of types relied on SFINAE (substitution failure is not an error), a clever but unreadable technique involving std::enable_if and template metaprogramming tricks. If you got it wrong, the compiler error was often a wall of template instantiation backtraces with no clear indication of what you did wrong.
Concepts (C++20) replace most of that with a named, readable constraint you can attach directly to a template parameter:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <concepts>

template <typename T>
concept Numeric = std::integral<T> || std::floating_point<T>;

template <Numeric T>
T square(T value) {
    return value * value;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << square(5) << "\n";     // fine, int is Numeric
    std::cout << square(2.5) << "\n";   // fine, double is Numeric
    // square("hi"); // clean compile error: does not satisfy Numeric, not a wall of SFINAE noise
}
```

If you're writing a template that only makes sense for certain kinds of types, use a concept to say so directly, rather than letting a misuse fail deep inside the implementation with a confusing error. Concepts are a large usability improvement, and by now the reasonable default whenever you'd have reached for SFINAE in older code.
Try it yourself: write a constrained template›
Write a template function clamp(value, low, high) constrained to only accept types satisfying std::totally_ordered (a standard concept meaning the type supports all six comparison operators), then try calling it with a type that doesn't satisfy it and read the resulting error.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <concepts>
#include <algorithm>

template <std::totally_ordered T>
T clampValue(T value, T low, T high) {
    return std::max(low, std::min(value, high));
}

int main() {
    std::cout << clampValue(15, 0, 10) << "\n"; // clamps to 10
    std::cout << clampValue(-5, 0, 10) << "\n"; // clamps to 0
}
```

C++, End to End
A C++ program is a set of text files the compiler turns into a single executable in two separate stages, and understanding that split saves you a lot of confusion later when an error message doesn't make sense. First, each .cpp file (a translation unit) gets compiled on its own into an object file. Then a separate program, the linker, stitches all the object files together into one executable, resolving which function definition goes with which function call across files.
Execution always starts in a function named main. Every C++ program needs exactly one. It returns an int: 0 conventionally means success, anything else means failure, and the operating system or calling script can check that return value.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello, world.\n";
    return 0;
}
```
A few things to notice in that example. #include <iostream> isn't a C++ statement, it's a preprocessor directive (the preprocessor gets its own chapter later), and it pulls in the declarations needed for std::cout. The std:: prefix means "look inside the std namespace," which is where almost everything the standard library gives you lives. And return 0; is technically optional in main specifically (the compiler inserts an implicit return 0; if you fall off the end), but write it anyway. Relying on that special case reads as an accident, not a decision.

std::cout writes to standard output, std::cin reads from standard input, and both use the << / >> operators (overloaded for this purpose, which is a preview of operator overloading, covered much later). Chain them to output or read multiple things in one statement.
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int age{};
    cout << "How old are you? ";
    cin >> age;
    cout << "You'll be " << age + 1 << " next year.\n";
}
```
That example uses using namespace std; to drop the std:: prefix everywhere. Don't do this in real code, especially not in a header file where it leaks into every file that includes it. It's fine in a 10-line teaching example; it's a real liability once a codebase has thousands of names in scope and two libraries happen to define something with the same name.

An int assigned to a double converts automatically (safe: no information is lost). A double assigned to an int also converts automatically, but truncates the fractional part, which is a narrowing conversion: information can be lost, and the compiler usually still allows it with at most a warning.
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    double d = 3.99;
    int i = d; // implicit narrowing: i becomes 3, not 4, no rounding
    std::cout << i << "\n";

    int small = 300;
    char c = small; // char is often 8-bit signed: this can overflow silently
    std::cout << static_cast<int>(c) << "\n";
}
```
Brace initialization (int i{d}; instead of int i = d;) refuses to compile narrowing conversions at all, catching this class of bug at compile time. Prefer braces for exactly this reason when the value isn't already known to fit.

reinterpret_cast<T>(value): reinterprets the raw bits of one type as another, unrelated type. Legitimately needed sometimes (low-level bit manipulation, interfacing with hardware or C APIs), but it bypasses the type system's safety entirely.
The old C-style cast (int)x still compiles and silently picks whichever of the four above would apply, which is exactly the problem: reading (SomeType)value tells you nothing about which of those four very different operations is happening. The C++ casts are more to type, but they're grep-able, and each one documents its own intent at the call site.
```
#include <iostream>

struct Base { virtual ~Base() = default; };
struct Derived : Base { void hello() { std::cout << "derived hello\n"; } };

int main() {
    Base* b = new Derived();
    if (Derived* d = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b)) {
        d->hello();
    }
    delete b;

    double pi = 3.14159;
    int truncated = static_cast<int>(pi);
    std::cout << truncated << "\n";
}
```

Both typedef and the newer using create an alternate name for an existing type. They're equivalent in what they can express for simple cases, but using also handles templated aliases, which typedef can't do at all.
```
#include <cstdint>

typedef std::uint32_t old_style_alias;
using new_style_alias = std::uint32_t;

template<typename T>
using Vec2 = std::pair<T, T>; // typedef literally cannot express this

int main() {
    Vec2<int> point{3, 4};
    return 0;
}
```
Prefer using in new code. It reads left-to-right like a normal declaration (using Name = Type;), and you'll eventually want a templated alias somewhere, at which point typedef simply isn't an option.

auto asks the compiler to deduce a variable's type from its initializer. It's not "dynamic typing", the type is fixed at compile time exactly as if you'd spelled it out, auto just saves you from spelling it. The rules have a few sharp edges worth knowing:
auto x = expr; deduces a plain value type, dropping top-level const and references. If expr is a const int&, x is just int.
auto& x = expr; deduces a reference, and top-level const is preserved this time (const auto& explicitly keeps it and additionally allows binding to temporaries).
auto deduces the *declared* type of the initializer, not necessarily what you'd guess. auto x = {1, 2, 3}; deduces std::initializer_list<int>, not an array or vector, a common surprise.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3};

    auto a = v[0];       // int (copy, const and reference both dropped)
    auto& b = v[0];      // const int& (reference preserved, and it was const to begin with)
    const auto& c = v[0]; // const int&, explicit and safest default for read-only access

    std::cout << a << ' ' << b << ' ' << c << "\n";
}
```

Where auto deduces a type from a value you're initializing with, decltype(expr) gives you back the exact type of an expression without evaluating it or requiring an initializer, useful in template code and when declaring a variable whose type should exactly match another variable or expression, including its reference-ness and const-ness (which plain auto would strip).
```
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    int& ref = x;
    decltype(ref) another_ref = x; // decltype(ref) is int&, exactly, unlike auto
    another_ref = 10;
    std::cout << x << "\n"; // 10, because another_ref really is a reference to x
}
```
